kernel32: add UnregisterApplicationRecoveryCallback (Lightroom's relaunch showed a crash dialog)

Lightroom Classic's "Reset all preferences and relaunch" showed Adobe's crash dialog over the
relaunched app; the app kept working until the dialog was closed (GitHub issue Neutron#5, reported
by the user and reproduced in the clean room). The log had one line:

    wine: Call from 00006FFFFFFA0CEA to unimplemented function
    KERNEL32.dll.UnregisterApplicationRecoveryCallback, aborting

Lightroom calls it before relaunching itself. kernel32 exported RegisterApplicationRecoveryCallback
(a stub that fakes success) but not its counterpart, so the call aborted and the crash handler
caught it. This adds the counterpart, faking success the same way. Normal quits never call it (none
of the user's daily Lightroom logs has the line).

Verified with a test program that registers and unregisters a recovery callback: before, the same
abort (exception 80000100); after, S_OK. In Lightroom Classic (clean room), reset-and-relaunch no
longer shows the dialog and the relaunched app works.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

--- a/dlls/kernel32/kernel32.spec
+++ b/dlls/kernel32/kernel32.spec
@@ -1586,7 +1586,7 @@
 @ stdcall -import UnlockFileEx(long long long long ptr)
 @ stdcall -import UnmapViewOfFile(ptr)
 @ stdcall -import UnmapViewOfFileEx(ptr long)
-# @ stub UnregisterApplicationRecoveryCallback
+@ stdcall UnregisterApplicationRecoveryCallback()
 @ stdcall UnregisterApplicationRestart()
 # @ stub UnregisterConsoleIME
 @ stdcall UnregisterWait(long)
--- a/dlls/kernel32/process.c
+++ b/dlls/kernel32/process.c
@@ -598,6 +598,20 @@
     return S_OK;
 }
 
+/**********************************************************************
+ *           UnregisterApplicationRecoveryCallback     (KERNEL32.@)
+ *
+ * NEUTRON (GitHub issue Neutron#5): Lightroom Classic calls this before it relaunches itself
+ * ("Reset all preferences and relaunch"). It was not exported at all, so the call aborted and
+ * Adobe's crash dialog appeared over the relaunched app. Pairs with the registration above,
+ * which already fakes success.
+ */
+HRESULT WINAPI UnregisterApplicationRecoveryCallback(void)
+{
+    FIXME(": stub, faking success\n");
+    return S_OK;
+}
+
 static SYSTEM_LOGICAL_PROCESSOR_INFORMATION_EX *get_logical_processor_info(void)
 {
     DWORD size = 0;
